// NEGATIVE CONTROL for `// EXPECT_ERR:`. The runner must report FAIL for this
// file; the self-test block inverts the verdict.
//
// The construction isolates EXPECT_ERR from the directive beside it. The program
// genuinely fails to compile (an empty `0x` literal), so `// EXPECT_FAIL:` is
// SATISFIED. The only thing that can make the runner report FAIL is the error
// pattern below, which no diagnostic in this compiler emits. If the runner
// reports anything other than FAIL, EXPECT_ERR is matching nothing.
//
// This control earned its place by catching exactly that. Before the runner
// stripped the compiler's echoed source window from the text it searches, this
// file reported PASS — the diagnostic renderer prints a window of lines around
// the error, so the `// EXPECT_ERR:` line itself appeared in the output and the
// pattern matched ITSELF. EXPECT_ERR was asserting nothing at all, and nothing
// else in the suite could have noticed.
//
// The pattern is deliberately a string no diagnostic could ever contain, and it
// appears nowhere in this file outside its own directive line — the runner
// rejects a pattern that occurs in the test's own non-directive source, because
// the echoed window would satisfy such a pattern even with the rule deleted.
// EXPECT_FAIL:
// EXPECT_ERR: no_diagnostic_in_this_compiler_emits_this_text
var a = 0x
println("${a}")
